    "The script's primary weakness is its critical and complex dependency on an external helper script (`ALCF/helpers.sh`) and its functions (`setEnv`, `ezpz`, `setParams`, etc.). The script's note correctly identifies that these helpers, originally written for a PBS environment, must be completely and correctly adapted to use Flux environment variables (e.g., `FLUX_JOB_NNODES`, `flux resource list`). If this adaptation is flawed, many essential variables (`$LR`, `$SEQ`, `$CKPT_DIR`, `$OUTPUT_LOG`, etc.) needed for the final `deepspeed` command will be unset, causing the job to fail.",
    "The script uses a login shell (`#!/bin/bash --login`), which is generally not recommended for batch jobs. Login shells source user profile scripts (like `.bash_profile`), which can introduce unexpected environment variables, aliases, or functions that may conflict with the batch environment.",
    "The entire `deepspeed` command is constructed as a single, large string (`run_cmd`) and executed with `eval`. This approach can make the script very difficult to debug, as it obscures quoting issues and variable expansion errors."
